    "type": "question",
    "label": "77",
    "prompt": "Show that $\\dfrac{\\log_a(c)}{\\log_{ab}(c)}=1+\\log_a(b)$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$\\dfrac{\\log_a(c)}{\\log_{ab}(c)}=1+\\log_a(b)$.\n\nChange the denominator to base $a$, cancel and apply the product law.",
      "worked": "For $a,b,c>0$, $a\\ne1$, $ab\\ne1$ and $c\\ne1$, change the denominator to base $a$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{\\log_a(c)}{\\log_{ab}(c)}&=\\frac{\\log_a(c)}{\\dfrac{\\log_a(c)}{\\log_a(ab)}}\\\\\\frac{\\log_a(c)}{\\log_{ab}(c)}&=\\log_a(ab)\\\\\\frac{\\log_a(c)}{\\log_{ab}(c)}&=\\log_a(a)+\\log_a(b)\\\\\\frac{\\log_a(c)}{\\log_{ab}(c)}&=1+\\log_a(b).\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 48
  },
